  "apod": {
    "date": "2026-11-28",
    "title": "Apollo 17 VIP Site Anaglyph",
    "explanation": " Explanation:  Get out your red/blue glasses and check out this stereo scene from  Taurus-Littrow  valley on the Moon! The color anaglyph features a detailed 3D view of Apollo 17's  Lunar Rover  in the foreground. Behind it lies the Lunar Module and distant lunar hills. Because the world was going to be able  to watch  the  Lunar Module's  ascent stage liftoff via the rover's TV camera, this  parking place  was also known as the VIP Site.  Fifty years ago , in December of 1972, Apollo 17 astronauts Eugene Cernan and Harrison Schmitt spent about 75 hours on the Moon, while colleague Ronald Evans  orbited overhead . The crew returned with 110 kilograms of rock and soil samples, more than from any of the other lunar landing sites. Cernan and Schmitt  are still  the last  to walk  ( or drive )  on the Moon .   Tomorrow's picture:  dark cloud",
    "image": "https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/a17anaglyph_vanMeijgaarden_f.jpg?w=2015&h=2172&fit=clip",
    "link": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-28-apollo-17-vip-site-anaglyph/",
    "credit": " Gene Cernan ,  Apollo 17 ,  NASA ; Anaglyph by  Erik van Meijgaarden ",
    "alt": "Lunar rover on the Moon.",
    "media_type": "image"
  },
  "updated": "2026-11-28T09:46:21.017296+00:00"
